  "blocks": [
    {
      "id": "lamed-block-1",
      "sourceIndex": 0,
      "text": "Lamed Hebrew Letter (ל): Sound, Words & Practice",
      "reviewFlags": [],
      "type": "MAIN_TITLE"
    },
    {
      "id": "lamed-block-2",
      "sourceIndex": 1,
      "text": "Lamed (ל) is the twelfth letter of the Hebrew alphabet. In modern Hebrew, it is pronounced [l], like the l in 'leaf'. You've now learned more than half of the 22 letters of the Hebrew alphabet!",
      "reviewFlags": [],
      "type": "SIMPLE_TEXT"
    },
    {
      "id": "lamed-block-4",
      "sourceIndex": 3,
      "text": "Words to Practice:",
      "reviewFlags": [],
      "type": "SUBTITLE"
    },
    {
      "id": "lamed-block-5",
      "sourceIndex": 4,
      "text": "heart",
      "reviewFlags": [],
      "type": "WORD_EXAMPLE",
      "word": "לֵב",
      "transcription": "lev"
    },
    {
      "id": "lamed-block-6",
      "sourceIndex": 5,
      "text": "The Hebrew word לֵב [lev] means 'heart'. It consists of two letters: Lamed (ל) and Bet (ב). Lamed gives [l]. In this word, Bet has no dagesh and gives [v], not [b]. Tzere, the two dots beneath Lamed, represents [e]. Read the sounds together as one syllable: [lev]. In text with pronunciation marks, בּ with a dagesh gives [b], while ב without a dagesh gives [v]. In ordinary writing without these marks, you need to know the word to choose the right sound.",
      "reviewFlags": [],
      "type": "BOLD_TEXT"
    },
    {
      "id": "lamed-block-7",
      "sourceIndex": 6,
      "text": "boy",
      "reviewFlags": [],
      "type": "WORD_EXAMPLE",
      "word": "יֶלֶד",
      "transcription": "yeled"
    },
    {
      "id": "lamed-block-8",
      "sourceIndex": 7,
      "text": "The Hebrew word יֶלֶד [yeled] means 'boy' or 'male child'. It consists of three letters: Yud (י), Lamed (ל), and Dalet (ד). Each Segol, the three-dot vowel sign beneath Yud and Lamed, represents [e]. Read the word in two syllables, [ye-led], with the stress on the first syllable. The final [d] ends the second syllable; it is not a syllable on its own. Also review לֶחֶם [lekhem], 'bread': [le-khem], with the stress on the first syllable. Here, kh represents one consonant sound, like the ch in German 'Bach'.",
      "reviewFlags": [],
      "type": "BOLD_TEXT"
    },
    {
      "id": "lamed-block-9",
      "sourceIndex": 8,
      "text": "Reading Practice",
      "reviewFlags": [],
      "type": "SUBTITLE"
